#### 1. Underscore() triple regex Replace chain + `.ToLower()` (1 instance) 🔴
**Impact:** 4 intermediate string allocations per call — called millions of times directly and transitively via `Kebaberize()`. `.ToLower()` is also culture-sensitive (correctness risk on Turkish-I).
**Files:** `CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L115-121`
**Fix:** Replace the 3-regex chain with a single-pass `StringBuilder` or `string.Create` approach that inserts underscores at boundaries and lowercases in one allocation. At minimum, change `.ToLower()` → `.ToLowerInvariant()`.

```csharp
// Example single-pass replacement
public static string Underscore(this string input)
{
    if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(input)) return input;
    var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length + 4);
    for (int i = 0; i < input.Length; i++)
    {
        char c = input[i];
        if (c == '-' || c == ' ')
        {
            sb.Append('_');
        }
        else if (char.IsUpper(c))
        {
            if (i > 0 && (char.IsLower(input[i - 1])
                || (i + 1 < input.Length && char.IsUpper(input[i - 1]) && char.IsLower(input[i + 1]))))
                sb.Append('_');
            sb.Append(char.ToLowerInvariant(c));
        }
        else
        {
            sb.Append(c);
        }
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}
```

#### 2. Pascalize() per-match `.Substring().ToUpper()` + concatenation (1 instance) 🔴
**Impact:** 3 string allocations per regex match (`.Substring(0,1)`, `.ToUpper()`, `.Substring(1)`, plus concat). With multiple word parts per input × millions of calls, this is a major allocation source. `Camelize()` compounds this by then allocating again via `pascalized[1..]`.
**Files:** `CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L78-79`, `CaseTransformExtensions.cs:L101`
